        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.639,
            0.049,
            0.846,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Constructions and Tilings"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.088,
            0.316,
            0.113
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.122,
            0.846,
            0.161
          ],
          "content": "1. Use support lines in Fig. 6.11 to construct a pointed arch. Make different arches, by changing the radius of the arcs."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.166,
            0.478,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": "2. Make your own arch designs."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.204,
            0.377,
            0.227
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Regular Hexagons"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.23,
            0.848,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": "Recall that a regular polygon has equal sides and equal angles. A regular polygon with 3 sides is an equilateral triangle, and a regular polygon with 4 sides is a square. We have constructed these figures earlier."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.303,
            0.812,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": "? How do we construct a regular pentagon (5-sided figure) and a regular hexagon (6-sided figure)? To begin with, try to construct a pentagon and hexagon with equal sidelengths."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.295,
            0.927,
            0.366
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.551,
            0.848,
            0.611
          ],
          "content": "To construct a regular pentagon, we first need to have a better understanding of triangles and pentagons. We will discuss this in later years. However, constructing a regular hexagon is within our reach!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.618,
            0.846,
            0.658
          ],
          "content": "？ Can we break a regular hexagon into smaller pieces that can be constructed?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.674,
            0.603,
            0.695
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Regular Hexagon and Equilateral Triangles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.699,
            0.664,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": "What happens when we join the ‘opposite’ points of a regular hexagon? Since a regular hexagon has equal sides and angles, can we expect a figure like this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.762,
            0.663,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "Will all the triangles in the figure be equilateral triangles?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.672,
            0.669,
            0.839,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.801,
            0.663,
            0.84
          ],
          "content": "To answer these questions, we will reverse our approach."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.721,
            0.821,
            0.79,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 6.12"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.848,
            0.783,
            0.89
          ],
          "content": "？ Can six congruent equilateral triangles be placed together as in Fig. 6.12? If yes, will it result in a regular hexagon?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.807,
            0.933,
            0.843,
            0.95
          ],
          "content": "151"
        }
      ]
    }
